<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    The portable presentation layer: view-models, flow state machines, and the display logic that decides what
    a surface says — with no UI framework anywhere in it.

    Why this exists as its own assembly rather than living in Ripcord.App: Ripcord.App targets
    net10.0-windows and pulls in WinUI, so nothing in it can be linked by the planned macOS/Linux/tvOS front
    ends. Each of those is to get a native UI for its own OS, which only works if the layer beneath the UI is
    genuinely UI-agnostic.

    Why not fold this into Ripcord.Client: that project's charter is the session lifecycle and it references
    only Ripcord.Core and Ripcord.Diagnostics. Everything here needs something it deliberately does not have
    (the paired-console store, a dispatcher seam, discovery/registration seams), and merging the two would
    drag view-model concerns into the assembly whose whole value is having none. Referencing it is the right
    shape, and the edge points downward.

    THE RULE: no UI framework types cross this boundary — no Brush, no Color, no Visibility, no
    DispatcherQueue. Presentation concerns are expressed as portable enums and bools that each front end maps
    to its own types. PresentationPortabilityTests enforces this, because prose alone has not held elsewhere
    in this repo.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Ripcord.Core\Ripcord.Core.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Ripcord.Client\Ripcord.Client.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Ripcord.Diagnostics\Ripcord.Diagnostics.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
